---
description: sizeColumnsToFit was called while the grid had no width, usually because it was not visible yet.
---

## Grid Had No Width to Fit

`sizeColumnsToFit()` divides the grid's available width between the columns, so it needs the grid to have a width. This message means the grid body measured zero pixels wide, and the columns were left at their existing widths.

The grid does not give up on the first attempt. It re-measures immediately, then after 100ms, then after 500ms, and only warns once all four attempts come back at zero. **Wrapping your own call in a longer `setTimeout` is very unlikely to help** — that is the first thing most people try, and the grid has already done it for you.

A grid measuring zero wide almost always means it is not on screen, or its container has not been given a size.

### Check the container has a size

The grid fills its container, so the container needs a height and width of its own. A container with no explicit size, or one whose parent has collapsed, gives the grid nothing to fill. See [Grid Size](./grid-size/) for the ways to size it.

### Check the grid is visible when you call

This is the common case: the call runs while the grid is hidden, so there is nothing to measure.

- **In a tab, accordion, modal or drawer** that is not the active one. Anything hidden with `display: none` has zero size. Call `sizeColumnsToFit()` when the panel becomes visible, not when it is created.
- **Before the container has been laid out**, for example immediately after mounting rather than from a grid event.
- **In a collapsed flex or grid child**, where the parent has given the column no width.

Note that `visibility: hidden` and `opacity: 0` still occupy space, so those are safe; `display: none` is not.

### Prefer declaring the strategy over calling the API

For the usual case of "fit the columns when the grid loads", the [`autoSizeStrategy`](./column-sizing/#auto-size-columns-to-fit-grid) grid option is a better fit than an imperative call, because the grid applies it at the right moment itself:

```{% frameworkTransform=true spaceBetweenProperties=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    autoSizeStrategy: {
        type: 'fitGridWidth',
    },
}
```

If you want the columns to keep sharing the width as the grid resizes, use [Column Flex](./column-sizing/#column-flex) instead. `flex` is maintained by the grid continuously, so it survives the container being hidden and shown again without any call from you.

### If you must call it yourself

Call it once the grid is genuinely visible. Reacting to the container becoming visible — a tab-change handler, or a `ResizeObserver` on the container — is more reliable than a delay, because it does not depend on guessing how long layout takes.
